              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ゆうびんきょく — yūbinkyoku — \"post office\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ゆうびんきょくは えきの ちかくです。 — yūbinkyoku wa eki no chikaku desu — \"The post office is near the station.\""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ゆうびんきょく (yūbinkyoku) is Sino-Japanese (kango): three Chinese characters read with their on'yomi, readings borrowed from Chinese. They are yū, \"post station\" (Middle Chinese hjuw), bin, \"convenience\" (bjienH) and kyoku, \"office, bureau\" (gjowk) (KANJIDIC2; Middle Chinese in Baxter's transcription)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for everyday life at A2."
